# Repair plan for /go-test/go-test:fixture.stepsrun.tests.calc::TestCalc001Double

## Summary

One production defect, one file, inside the declared edit root `services/calc`. The
calculator's `double` command parses its operand and then prints the operand back
unchanged instead of printing twice its value. No test, step definition, document or
assertion needs to change, and no undeclared path is required.

## Evidence

- `services/calc/calc.go:7-8` — the program does `value, err := strconv.Atoi(os.Args[2])`
  and then `fmt.Println(value)`. The doubling is simply absent: whatever integer comes in
  on the argument goes straight back out on stdout.
- `tests/calc/steps/register.go:12-20` (`installed`, the Given/`installation` concept)
  builds `./services/calc` with `go build -o ../../tmp/calc-program/calc` from the host
  root and keeps the absolute program path in `state.Program`. So the binary under test is
  built fresh from this source on every run — there is no stale-artifact explanation for
  the failure, and the existing `tmp/calc-program/calc` will be overwritten.
- `tests/calc/steps/register.go:23-29` (`doubling`, the When concept) runs
  `state.Program double 3`, trims the output and parses it into `state.Value`.
- `tests/calc/steps/register.go:32-35` (`result`, the Then concept) compares `state.Value`
  with the expected `6` and reports `expected %d, got %d`.
- Observed failure `expected 6, got 3` is exactly the signature of the program echoing its
  input: the harness, the parse and the comparison are all correct, and the integer that
  arrives is the un-doubled operand.

## Change to make

### File: `services/calc/calc.go` (the only file to touch)

Make the `double` command print twice the parsed operand instead of the operand itself.
The single `fmt.Println(value)` call on line 8 is the defect; it must emit the doubled
integer.

Intended contract after the change, which must match `docs/architecture/calc-cli.yaml`
and the product glossary:

- Invocation shape stays `calc double VALUE`.
- On a well-formed invocation the program writes the doubled integer followed by a single
  newline to stdout and exits 0. Per the glossary term `number` ("An integer operand whose
  double is its sum with itself"), the doubled integer is the operand added to itself —
  compute it as `value + value` so the code states the definition the specification gives,
  rather than relying on an unrelated multiplication idiom.
- Nothing else about the output may change: exactly one line, no label, no prefix, no extra
  whitespace, because the When step does `strconv.Atoi(strings.TrimSpace(...))` on the whole
  captured output and any additional text would break the parse.

Behaviour that must be preserved exactly as it is today — do not rewrite or "tidy" these,
as the CLI document pins them:

- `len(os.Args) != 3` or `os.Args[1] != "double"` still exits 1 (`'1': Invalid arguments`).
- A non-integer operand (`strconv.Atoi` error) still exits 1.
- The program stays `package main` in `services/calc`, buildable by
  `go build -o <path> ./services/calc` from the host root, since the Given step depends on
  that exact build invocation.
- Keep the file free of new imports; `fmt`, `os` and `strconv` already cover the work.

